The difference between start() and run() is a classic Java multithreading question 🔥
I’ll explain it clearly, with examples, and interview-ready.

1️⃣ What is run()?

run() is a normal method

It contains the code (task) to execute

Calling run() does NOT create a new thread

Example
class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread running");
    }
}


Calling:

MyThread t = new MyThread();
t.run();

What happens?

Runs in main thread

No multithreading

Just a method call

2️⃣ What is start()?

start() creates a new thread

JVM calls run() internally

Executes run() in a separate thread

Example
MyThread t = new MyThread();
t.start();

What happens?

New thread created

run() executed asynchronously

True multithreading

3️⃣ Core Difference (Side-by-Side)
| Feature            | `run()`       | `start()`       |
| ------------------ | ------------- | --------------- |
| Creates new thread | ❌ No          | ✅ Yes           |
| Calls `run()`      | ❌ Direct call | ✅ JVM calls     |
| Thread execution   | Main thread   | Separate thread |
| Asynchronous       | ❌ No          | ✅ Yes           |
| Used for           | Normal method | Multithreading  |

4️⃣ Output Example (Very Important)
class Test extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t1 = new Test();
        Test t2 = new Test();

        t1.run();
        t2.start();
    }
}

Possible Output
main
Thread-0

5️⃣ Interview Trap Question ⚠️
❓ Can we call start() twice?
t.start();
t.start(); // ❌

Answer:

🚫 No

Throws IllegalThreadStateException

6️⃣ Correct Usage Pattern
Best Practice
new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Running in new thread");
}).start();

7️⃣ One-Line Interview Answer

run() executes code like a normal method, while start() creates a new thread and then invokes run() internally.

🟢 Summary

Never call run() to start a thread

Always call start() for multithreading